ed segment by segment and without regard to letter case, so `secrets/a.txt` and `SECRETS/a.txt` are closed alike, whether or not the root holds the folder, while `secrets-notes.md` and `a/secrets/b` are not. Its default, `closed_paths: [secrets]`, is written in `config/behavior.yml`. A `closed_paths` that is absent, or a value that is not a list of strings, gives the finding `findings.apps.closed_paths_shape`, which names the file `config/behavior.yml` and the key `apps.closed_paths`, as `behaviorFindings` reports the other errors of that file (`src/lib/data/sources/config/catalogs.ts:152`), and while it stands every `file` target rejects `closed` for every app, so an error in the key closes files and never opens one; an entry that is empty, starts or ends with `/`, or holds an empty, `.`, `..` or dot-led segment gives the same finding and is left out, the other entries applying; a duplicate entry applies once and gives no finding. `config/behavior.yml` has no `apps:` block until the build step adds it with every key of the block and its value. A `file` whose `path` breaks the segment rule of the app file server above rejects `closed` for every app, a machine app and a root app alike: a segment that is empty, `.`, `..` or dot-led, or a backslash or NUL anywhere. So `.env`, `.ssh/id_ed25519`, `.oculus/approvals.json`, `a/.git/config`, `a//b`, `/etc/hosts` (its leading segment is empty), `a/./b` and an empty path each reject `closed`, and a dot inside a segment (`notes.v2.md`) is not dot-led. The rule is checked on the exact string the app passed: `open` takes a string over the bridge and decodes nothing, so `%2Eenv` is a literal name that does not start with a dot, and the page passes the path URL-encoded to the source route, which decodes it back to the checked string. The rule and `closed_paths` are applied again to the part of the file's real path under the root's real path, so a link inside a root that points at a dot folder (`creds` pointing at `.secrets`, opened as `creds/key`) rejects `closed`, and so does a link whose target lies outside the root, whether or not the target exists. The real-path checks compare the real path defined under "Where an app lives", so `hub` with `TOOLS/OCULUS/CONFIG/local.yml` rejects `closed` and, on a root holding `secrets/`, `SECRETS/a.txt` rejects `closed` under the default `closed_paths`. The checks run in this order, so that the answer for a hidden path never depends on the disk or on which roots are shown: the segment rule and `closed_paths` on the given path; the key `engine`; for a root app, a root other than its own (`closed`); whether the root is shown (`missing`); the checks on the real path, each `closed` (outside the root, inside oculus's own `config/` folder, the segment rule and `closed_paths` on the part under the root); then whether the file exists, and a file that does not exist rejects `missing`. A binary file, and a file that `open.program_ext` maps to a program such as a `.docx`, open in the source tab as source text, as every file `open` names does, and no program is started. Every check of `{ file }` after the view and project forms runs on the server, since the page cannot see a real path: before it opens the tab the page calls `GET /api/app-open?app=<view id>&root=<root key>&path=<path>`, each parameter sent through `encodeURIComponent`, which runs the checks above in their order for that app and answers `{ result: "ok" | "closed" | "missing" }`; the page opens the source tab only on `ok` and otherwise rejects the word the route answered. The route sits beside `GET /api/app-store` under the same rules: GET only (any other method answers 405), it writes nothing, a parameter that is absent, empty or given twice answers 400, an `app` no held row names answers 404, and only the page calls it. The bridge's `open` reaches the page's own tab opener and never `POST /api/open`, the route that starts a program (the open action of the Configuration section), which stays closed t