 - The data layer's finding sentences are catalog text. Every sentence a source reader, a validation rule or `loadConfig` reports is a key of `config/text/findings.yml` (`findings.<source>.*`, the sentences several readers share under `findings.file.*`, the shape words under `findings.shape.*`), worded on the server by `findingText(catalog, key, ...args)` of `data/helpers/finding-text.ts`, which the audit scan also words its findings with and whose `fillPlaceholders` is what `t()` fills a string with. The readers take the catalog as a parameter and the rules as `ValidationRules.text`, both from `PortfolioConfig.text`; the wire shape of a finding is unchanged. `findingText` never throws: a key the catalog lacks is written as the key followed by its arguments, so a sentence file that does not parse costs the wording and never the portfolio, a document read or a write, and a missing `config/` folder, which has no catalog at all, is always shown as `findings.config.directory_missing`. A key misspelt in the code is caught by `tests/checks/source/keys.test.ts`, which holds every `findings.` literal under `src` to the file, every key of the file to a literal, and every `findingText` key that is not a whole literal to a documented rule (the audit's collector).
 - The boards and collection cards. A card's controls are `views/shared/card/CardActions.svelte`: drawn under the pointer or keyboard focus of their own card, which `ui/frame/Card.svelte` says through `--card-reveal` on every card so a card inside another answers for itself, transparent otherwise so nothing moves and Tab reaches them, kept on one line with the ones that do not fit in one More menu drawn at the controls' height. The room is measured in fractional widths within the part of the card the pane shows, measured again when the card, its line or a box that clips it resizes or scrolls; the controls sit beside the chips when all of them or More alone fits there and on a line of their own from the line's start otherwise (`card-layout.ts`), and a measurement landing after a newer one or after the component is gone is dropped. A ticker row's buttons are the small text button, 22 px high, in the words the caller wrote, and are revealed the same way by `ui/display/Ticker.svelte`'s `reveal`, the button the row exists for (`kept`: a subtask's or follow-up's done, a task's `done` status) always drawn. On a device with a finger and a pointer, the first tap draws the hidden buttons at the row's end and cuts the name where they begin, the next tap presses what it lands on, and a tap away closes them; the row a tap opened is held by its stable write target so a write that moves the rows cannot open a different one, a pointer arriving over another row closes it, and a row with no hidden button takes one tap. A long description is `views/shared/card/Clamp.svelte`, four lines, its open state `more` in the view instance's state. The board's facets are one `ui/menu/Menu` whose `groups` draw a heading per facet (`state/boards/board/filter-menu.ts`). Card grids stretch each row's cards to one height in file order, and a card breaks a long unbroken title inside its width. A card draws no subtasks block until it has a subtask, adding the first being a card control. The project tab draws its documents once, present Markdown only, the card's list left out through `ProjectCard`'s `documents`, and a known document the project has not got as the tag the card draws for it. The Map hides a label overlapping one drawn before it (`views/structure/map/labels.ts`), the hovered and selected labels drawn last and an archived node's label dimmed. Lit Watch's grid is one column of `minmax(0, 1fr)`, so a long line never widens the pane. The Memories wall draws every tile at its picture's proportions within `TILE_BOUND`, the whole picture fitted inside, and a picture that failed is asked for again, at an address of its own, when the folder answers again.
 - The awaiting strip counts every live project and every project a lane draws, and lane headers on the open board are told apart. `waiting()` tallies the people and venues of every live project, whether or not a card draws it, and of every project of another tier that `laneItems` draws on a lane; a project of another tier in holding or on no lane is not counted. A lane header's surface is half its hue and half the panel (`LANE_SHARE` in `themes/derive.ts`). The lane colours are Run green, Push blue, Revise amber, Review slate, Blocked red, Backburner grey, Scope cyan-d, De-risk honey, Build teal, with the Ideas lane mauve and the columns Funding gold, Jobs grey-d and Teaching teal. With every cluster open and every column drawn, every pair of headers that touch (next in drawing order, stacked in one column, beside the full-width Ideas lane) stays a CIEDE2000 of 10 or more apart under every theme. A collapsed cluster or a hidden column brings other headers together, and no colours keep all of them apart: seven headers can all touch one another, and at this share no more than five hues are pairwise 10 apart under every theme. A header's text colour, `--c-<hue>-lane-txt`, is measured only on its own lane surface.
 - Showing a path in the file browser. `layout.showInFiles(path, root = 'hub')` activates the Files tab of the page the reader is in, or opens one there; a Files tab on another page is left alone. A maximised pane that holds no Files tab is restored first, so the page's own Files tab is found, and a maximised pane that holds one keeps the reader's layout and its Files tab is used. It writes an ask `{ root, path, asked, settled }` into that instance's state, clears its filter, and normalises the path by segments through `state/boards/collection.ts`'s `normalisePath`. `views/shared/explorer/tree/ShowPath.svelte` reads the folders on the way outermost first through the fs store's `readListing`, one request per folder per round shared with the render. It writes the open ancestors, the selection and the outcome only while the instance still holds that ask, so an answer after a later ask, after the tab closed or after another mount settled it writes nothing. The row is scrolled into view once drawn, takes the keyboard, and the ask is cleared. A path not there selects the nearest folder on its way and the outcome line says so; an unreadable folder and a root the workspace lacks are said the same way. An ask made while the roots could not be read is answered at once with that failure, and that line is cleared once the roots read. The control appears on the project card beside the Finder control, on a collection card for each `link`, `url` or `folder` value that `hubPathsOf` in `state/boards/collection.ts` counts as a hub path (not an address and not climbing above the hub), on a venue card's `link` by the same rule (its site link opening the hub file in a tab of its own, as a collection card's does), and on the Person view's supervision folder by the same rule.
-- Tabs, pages and panes drawn like VS Code's editor. `ui/tabs/Tabs.svelte` draws the strip and `ui/tabs/TabFace.svelte` one tab in it, both at the `--tab-*` tokens of `themes/tokens.css` (a 35 px tab, 10 px lead, 28 px close room and a 20 px close box, grown with `--text-scale`, and `--tabs-least`, 120 px, the least the tabs get on a line: a strip too narrow for that width and the two scroll controls draws no scroll controls (`controlsFit` of `ui/tabs/tab-strip.ts`), and the strip's buttons that would leave the tabs less move to a line of their own, so in a narrow pane or at a large text size the shown tab keeps its title and its close control): a line between tabs, the shown tab on `--panel` over a gap in the line under the strip, its top line in `--accent` only when the strip is `focused` and in `--line` otherwise. `shell/panes/PaneView.svelte` passes `focused` to the pane holding `openingInstance()`, the active pane of the page the reader is in, so one pane strip says where a view opens even when another page is drawn inside it; `shell/panes/leaf/FoldedPane.svelte` draws the same accent on that pane's folded strip. A `Tab.dirty` tab, set from `ui.unsaved` for a pane tab, shows the `bullet` glyph in the close control's place and is named `ui.tab_unsaved`; the face, its marks and its close control are `ui/tabs/TabFace.svelte`'s, and the strip, its scrolling, its focus order and its drag are `ui/tabs/Tabs.svelte`'s. The strip's scroll controls, the pane header's split-right button and its opener on `ui.pane_more_glyph` are `bare` ToolButton and Menu boxes of `--tab-action`, the same in a terminal palette; `ui.pane_menu_glyph` stays the Sessions view's row menus' glyph. `ui/frame/Sash.svelte` draws every handle, the split's and the Source view's, with no line at rest, a `--sash-line` accent line after `--sash-delay` of hover and at once in a drag, a `--sash-focus-line` accent line for the keyboard in place of the browser's ring, and a `::before` reach over the neighbours' 1 px borders, `--sash-reach` after the handle and the sliver `--sash-reach-start` before it, since a pointer is hit-tested as the CSS pixel that starts at it, so no pane's last device pixel catches the handle at any ratio and `SASH_PX` and every size are unchanged. The label is 13 px (`--fs-md`), the close control and the pin 16 px (`--fs-xl`) and the unsaved dot 8 px, VS Code's measures; a strip that does not hold the focus draws its labels and its control at half strength. Kept different from VS Code: the sash measures in pixels not grown with the text, tab count badges, a folded pane's strip, the scroll controls in place of a thin 3 px scrollbar, and no shadow under a hovered tab. The tab colours of the two VS Code Modern themes are the ten tab keys of the installed VS Code's `dark_modern.json` and `light_modern.json`: seven of them are surfaces those theme blocks already set (`--panel`, `--bg`, `--line`, `--accent`), and the keys the surfaces do not give are the tokens `--tab-active-txt` (dark), `--tab-inactive-txt` and `--tab-active-border` (light) in `themes/themes.css`; the other themes keep their own colours. The pane strip is `shell/panes/strip/PaneStrip.svelte`, shaped by `strip-shape.ts` from the keys of `config/appearance.yml`, `show_tabs` (`multiple`, `single`, `none`) and `editor_actions` (`strip`, `hidden`), read through `state/shared/words/appearance.ts` and written by the Tab Bar and Editor Actions Position submenus of the strip menu (`stripMenu` of `shell/panes/pane-view.ts`, which also offers New Text File and Lock Group or Unlock Group); a locked pane draws a lock at the strip's right end that unlocks on a press. Both submenus mark the choice in force with a check; the first place of Editor Actions Position is named Tab Bar, or Title Bar while the tab bar is hidden, and Hidden takes the `...` off the pane of a float too. New Text File opens an untitled text in a source tab of that pane (`newTextFileOpener` of `layout/open/new-text-file.ts`