ce tab that is not read at a commit (`views/sources/source/StripActions.svelte`, handed up through `ui/tabs/tab-actions.svelte.ts`, which keeps the snippet through a keep or a walk because `handUp` hands it up on every run of its effect), then Split Right and `...`; no other tab hands anything up. Save, Revert, Blame and the rendered half are palette actions of the source tab (`views/sources/source/status/ask-perform.ts`), published whether or not the tab draws an editor and bound to Alt+Z for word wrap in the editor, and Cmd+S is the shell key action `save` (`shell/keys/keys.ts`, performed in `workbench-keys.svelte.ts` by an ask to the source tab on screen). The bookmark, Open in Browser Tab and Open in Program items are in the tab's right-click menu (`shell/panes/strip/tab-menu.ts`, performed with state calls in `tab-menu-state.svelte.ts`) and in the file row's menu (`views/shared/explorer/tree/menu/row-menu.ts`), and the line saying what a save did stays in the source tab, under the editor. The Files tree draws no breadcrumb. A Terminal tab is titled by `terminalTabTitle` of `state/sessions/terminal/terminal-lists.ts` (the reader's title, else the agent's word, else the project command's name, else the process tmux reports, else the word for a shell), which only the tab uses; the Sessions view's rows and the Processes view keep `terminalLabel` (title, else name), and a terminal's viewport line, and whether it sat at the end, are kept per terminal when its grid goes (`state/sessions/terminal/viewport.ts`), so Float and Return to Page leave the scrollback where it was. A dialog (`ui/frame/Modal.svelte`) and the Memories picture overlay are drawn into the document body through `portal` of `ui/shown.ts`, outside the area that holds the floats, so they stand over a float; a dialog takes the focus once its bindings are set (`focusOnOpen` of `ui/frame/quiet-focus.ts`), and the Memories overlay gives it back through `focusQuietly`. The page cancels the browser's own context menu everywhere an oculus menu does not open (`shouldCancel` of `shell/context-guard.ts`, one document `contextmenu` listener in `routes/+page.svelte`), and leaves it inside a text input, a textarea, a contenteditable element, the code editor and the terminal grid.
 - A form is answered about the entry the write made. A write of a collection entry answers with the id the file gives that entry after the write (`WriteOutcome.id`, set by `writeEntry` and `createEntry` from their own `entryId`, carried in the endpoint's JSON and read into `WriteAnswer`), so a page holding a form over that entry is told where the entry now is rather than reading the file's id rule a second time. `views/shared/forms/EntryForm.svelte` addresses an add form's second save by that id and carries a bookmark form onto the id a rename wrote, draft and all. A write that would set an entry's identity field to a value another entry of the same file already carries is refused, naming that field, so no file comes to hold two entries a write cannot tell apart.
 - One Repositories list over every root, and one shape rule for both git views. `views/shared/changes/repo-shape.ts` is where a root's git state comes to a drawing, for the Changes panel and the Repositories view alike: `repoShape(root, state)` answers which of six it is (`unplaced`, `reading`, `missing`, `none`, `failed`, `readable`), `shapeBadge(shape)` answers the few words a list row carries at its end (`repositories.pick_missing`, `pick_norepo`, `pick_failed`, and nothing for a repository that reads), and `shapeBadgeTone(shape)` answers red for missing, blue for no repository, amber for failed and no tone for readable. The list is over every root the `git` channel carries a state for (`state/sources/repositories/repositories.ts` `pickerRoots`), which is the file browser's roots and every session's workspace, not the repositories among them: a folder that is no repository and a folder the reader deleted are rows that say which they are rather than rows that vanish, and `repositories.empty` is drawn only when the channel carries no root at all. In the Repositories view a row's hover words are its path and the badge's own are git's reason, and `data-repo` is written by `views/sources/repositories/Repo.svelte` for the four drawings that come to one sentence, not by the row that reads. `GitState.repository` is false for a root git ran to its end and answered is no repository, for a `.git` naming a gitdir that is gone, for a root with no `.git` to be seen, and for a git that could not start; it is true for a root git refused for a reason of its own where a `.git` can be seen (a config it cannot parse, a file under `.git` it cannot open), so such a repository is drawn as one whose read failed rather than as no repository at all.
 - The status bar and notifications, drawn as VS Code's. `shell/status/StatusBar.svelte` runs the window's whole width under the rail, 22 px high (`--status-height`) with 12 px items (`--status-text`), both fixed at every text scale. Every item is a status `ToolButton` or `Menu` with an icon, short words and a tooltip. The left group is host, branch, sync, findings, HPC, usage and server; the right group is the source tab's caret, indentation, encoding, line ending and language, then live sessions, unsaved tabs, and the bell last (`shell/status/status-items.ts`). The host is the server's `os.hostname()` on the `config` channel's `ConfigPayload.host`, with its address. Branch and sync are the repository holding the active tab's file (`repoStatus`), or the hub's; the branch picker switches through `switchBranch`; the sync item pushes through `push` when the branch is only ahead and otherwise pulls and then pushes through `sync`, and a branch with no upstream draws Publish Branch in its place, which pushes it to a remote and makes that its upstream, listing the remotes when there are several. Each asks a confirm naming the upstream or the remote before git runs, and a write git refuses opens the Repositories view on the repository, whose line says why. While a source tab's blame is on, the blame item before the caret says who last changed the caret's line, when and in which commit, or Not Committed Yet, and a press opens that commit in the Repositories view's graph. The findings item is the count of the Findings view's list (the snapshot's findings and the config channel's), opening the Findings view as VS Code's Problems count opens Problems. The source view publishes its caret, indent, ending and language to `state/workbench/navigation/editor-status.svelte.ts`, keyed by instance, and performs what the bar asks; the palette's `:<number>` goes to a line and `:language` lists the picker's entries and then the languages: Auto Detect (the tab's chosen language cleared, so the language the `languages:` map gives applies), Configure File Association for the file's ending (`languageActions` of `state/workbench/navigation/status.ts`; a second list whose choice `associateEnding` writes into `sources.yml`'s `languages:` as a `config-value` write recorded in History) and Configure the language's settings (opens the Settings view on `editor.languages`), each language row carrying its name in brackets and the language in force marked Configured Language. Language and indent choices are kept in the tab's instance state. A right-click lists the items; the hidden set and Do Not Disturb are kept in the ui store. Notifications are `state/workbench/navigation/notices.svelte.ts`: toasts show 8 s over the bar's right end, the centre lists them newest first with Clear, Clear All and Do Not Disturb. Five sources post, each once per event (`notice-sources.ts`): a refused rail write, a session that starts waiting on a permission prompt or goes idle after its turn (`permission_prompt`, `idle_prompt`), each worded as what it waits for, an update task whose run date moved, a Live HPC job newly in `recent`, and any write refused or unanswered (`writes.onUnwritten`).
