--- a/tools/oculus/HELP.md
+++ b/tools/oculus/HELP.md
@@ -46,8 +46,8 @@ Inside a page:
 - **Wrapped tabs.** With `wrap_tabs: true` in `appearance.yml` (a toggle under Appearance in Settings, off by default) a strip of `show_tabs: multiple` puts the tabs it cannot fit on more rows instead of scrolling one row, and the pane's actions stay at the end of the last row; a tab dropped on a wrapped strip lands by the row under the pointer as well as by its place along the row. The key is ignored under `single` and `none`.
 - **Pinned tabs.** Pin in a tab's right-click menu, Pin Editor in the palette (listed with Cmd-K Shift+Enter, for the active tab of the pane that last held the focus), or a drag into the pinned tabs, moves a tab to the start of its pane's strip, where it shows a pin in place of its close control (a press on the pin unpins; a pinned tab holding unsaved text shows its dot). Unpin, Unpin Editor or a drag out of the pinned tabs puts it back. A pinned tab keeps its pin when its pane floats, when it moves to another page and when its pane splits, and stays open through Close Others, Close to the Right, Close Saved, Close All and the Close All of Open Editors. Delete on a focused pinned tab closes nothing and shows the first tab that is not pinned.
 - **The path over a file tab.** A tab showing a file (a source tab, a document, a PDF or another file view) has the file's path drawn under the strip as a breadcrumb in place of a file-name row: the root, each folder, the file. A press on the root or a folder shows the Files view at that folder, and a press on the file shows it in the Files view. With `show_tabs: single` the breadcrumb sits in the strip's row beside the tab, and with `none` it has a row of its own. The tab's tooltip holds the file's whole path with the home folder written `~`.
-- **A file tab's controls.** The active file tab's Save, Revert, star, Open in Program, Preview and Blame sit at the right end of the pane's strip, before Split Right and the `...` opener; the source tab has no row of controls of its own, and the line saying what a save did, or why a bookmark or an open was refused, stays under the editor.
-- **How a strip is drawn.** Tabs stand edge to edge at one height, the shown tab on the pane's surface. The shown tab of the one pane a view opens into carries an accent line along its top and every other pane strip carries a plain one. A page drawn inside another page carries the pane accent only while the reader's active instance is inside that page, and a folded pane a view opens into carries the accent along its folded strip. A tab's close control shows on the shown tab and under the pointer. A tab's label is 13 px, its close control and pin 16 px, and a pane strip that does not hold the focus is drawn dimmer than the one that does. A tab holding text no save has taken shows a dot in the close control's place until the pointer is over it, and a screen reader hears its name followed by ", unsaved"; the Pages view marks a page holding such a tab as unsaved. The right end of a pane's strip holds the active file tab's controls, then, while the pane shows a tab, a split-right button, and the pane menu's ... opener; they and a strip's scroll controls are one bare icon each. At a large text size or in a narrow pane the shown tab keeps its title and its close control: a strip too narrow for its scroll arrows beside the tabs draws none, and its actions move to a line under the tabs, before a title is cut, so a pane about 100 px wide still shows the shown tab and its close control. A handle between panes, and between the Source view's editor and preview, shows nothing until the pointer rests on it, draws its line at once while dragged or when the keyboard reaches it, and is caught on the borders beside it as well as in its gap.
